WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.767
-Welcome to "The Tonight Show."
Thank you for coming on.

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:05.033
-Thank you for having me.
-It's great to see you.

00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:08.234
As a former senator,
I was wondering what are

00:00:08.234 --> 00:00:11.200
your takeaways from the State of
the Union last night?

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.434
-Well, I thought Biden was
especially good on Ukraine.

00:00:14.434 --> 00:00:15.767
-Yeah.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:20.934
-He's put together this amazing
coalition of nations

00:00:20.934 --> 00:00:23.100
to stand up to Putin.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.767
And he actually brought together

00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:27.934
Republicans and Democrats
last night.

00:00:27.934 --> 00:00:30.601
They were standing, you know.

00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:34.901
It was really heartening to see
Republicans standing

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:38.334
when he called Putin
a menacing dictator,

00:00:38.334 --> 00:00:40.567
especially when a few days
earlier,

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:44.234
Trump had called Putin a genius.

00:00:44.234 --> 00:00:50.501
And so, you know, obviously the
situation there is really dire,

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:55.501
and you just have to give credit
to the people of Ukraine.

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:57.701
Our hearts are with them.
-Absolutely. Yeah.

00:00:57.701 --> 00:01:02.734
-And the unbelievable leadership
from their president,

00:01:02.734 --> 00:01:07.734
from Zelenskyy,
who is incredibly brave.

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.767
You know, he was a comedian.

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.267
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:15.434
And as you know, comedians are
the bravest people in the world.

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:17.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:21.267
Especially Jewish comedians
who went into politics.

00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:22.734
[ Laughter ]
-Wow, yeah.

00:01:22.734 --> 00:01:24.868
-That's me and Zelenskyy.
But anyway.

00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:26.267
-But anyways, back to me.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:28.133
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:33.167
-It was really palpable last
night in the chamber, I thought,

00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:34.834
the emotion about Ukraine.

00:01:34.834 --> 00:01:37.734
-You've been to a number of
State of the Union addresses.

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:39.901
What is it like to be
in the chamber?

00:01:39.901 --> 00:01:42.701
I mean, is it kind of --
-Well, it's amazing.

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:47.501
I first one I did --
went to was Barack Obama.

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:51.734
And it's just so moving.

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:57.234
To be part of this democratic --
the democracy,

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:59.300
it's just unbelievably moving.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.801
-It makes you proud,
yeah, to be an American.

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:06.000
And what was it like
when Trump was president?

00:02:08.634 --> 00:02:10.634
[ Laughter ]
-Um...

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:11.934
-Take your time.

00:02:11.934 --> 00:02:13.467
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.467 --> 00:02:14.667
-Not so moving.

00:02:14.667 --> 00:02:17.234
[ Laughter ]
-Okay, alright.

00:02:17.234 --> 00:02:21.167
-You know, the first time
he spoke to the joint session,

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:23.701
we have a --
we had a caucus lunch.

00:02:23.701 --> 00:02:26.300
I mean, the Democratic senators
meet for lunch.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:29.434
And a couple of my colleagues
were saying like,

00:02:29.434 --> 00:02:32.701
"Do we really have to
stand up and applaud

00:02:32.701 --> 00:02:35.868
when he says something like,
you know, hackneyed like,

00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:37.200
'Under Donald Trump,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.767
America will be the strongest
nation in the world.'

00:02:39.767 --> 00:02:40.968
Do we have to stand up?"

00:02:40.968 --> 00:02:43.367
And Chuck Schumer goes,
"Yes, we've got to be --

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:45.000
we've got to be polite."

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.367
And we didn't like it.

00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:52.367
And I said, "Well, you know,
we could stand up and applaud,

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:54.667
but we could do
the slow sarcastic clap."

00:02:54.667 --> 00:02:57.167
And you know that as a comedian.

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:59.934
But none of my other colleagues
are comedians, you see.

00:02:59.934 --> 00:03:02.834
And so I had to show them
what it was.

00:03:02.834 --> 00:03:05.667
And it's basically,
you know, this.

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:10.267
Trump says, "Under Trump,
every American will be able to

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:12.400
put food on the table,
roof over their kids' heads

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.767
and send them to college."

00:03:13.767 --> 00:03:17.334
And then it's just like --

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:24.801
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.801 --> 00:03:28.400
And so a number of my colleagues
wanted to do it.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.501
But Chuck said, "No, we can't.
We've got to be polite."

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:33.267
-You got to be polite.

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:36.033
But before you got
elected senator,

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:38.467
you had an amazing
comedy career.

00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:39.734
You become a senator.

00:03:39.734 --> 00:03:42.667
Do you feel any pressure
not to be funny?

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:44.167
-Yeah.

00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:48.367
You know, I won by 312 votes
in my first election

00:03:48.367 --> 00:03:49.868
and I got there late.

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:53.133
And my team said to me,
"Don't be funny.

00:03:53.133 --> 00:03:54.667
Do not be funny.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.334
Don't be funny on the floor,
on a floor speech.

00:03:57.334 --> 00:03:59.367
Don't be funny in hearings.

00:03:59.367 --> 00:04:00.634
Don't be funny to the press."

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:01.868
I said, "I got it.
Don't be funny."

00:04:01.868 --> 00:04:03.234
They said, "Don't be funny."

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:05.734
But I really didn't
internalize it.

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:06.934
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.934 --> 00:04:10.501
So, the first day I'm there,
I get sworn in.

00:04:10.501 --> 00:04:12.601
I go right to my new office.
I hadn't been there.

00:04:12.601 --> 00:04:16.167
I get this slip of paper
on my desk, and I learned that

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:20.400
one of my duties is to write

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.300
congratulatory notes
to my constituents.

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:28.334
And the first one is Ruth
Anderson of Marshall, Minnesota,

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.033
who's turning 110.

00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:31.100
-Wow.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.667
-So, I get my official
stationery.

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:37.767
I write down, "Dear Ruth,
you have a bright future."

00:04:37.767 --> 00:04:43.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.400
-That's very funny.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:46.801
-I thought so.

00:04:46.801 --> 00:04:49.934
And so, my new assistant
picks it up

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:51.534
and takes it to
my new chief of staff.

00:04:51.534 --> 00:04:54.534
And he's in like in two seconds,
"What is this?!"

00:04:54.534 --> 00:04:56.400
-Yeah.
-I said, "It's a joke."

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.968
And he goes, "Uh-huh.

00:04:57.968 --> 00:05:01.734
You think Ruth Anderson
will find it funny?"

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:06.167
And I said, "Well, I don't know,
she's 110."

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:08.767
He said, "Well, you think her
family will find it funny?"

00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:10.033
And I went, "Oh, yeah,"

00:05:10.033 --> 00:05:14.133
and I started thinking about
her 90-year-old son.

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:21.400
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.734
-Yeah, he might be bummed out,
yeah.

00:05:23.734 --> 00:05:25.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.367 --> 00:05:28.367
-"You've got a bright future"?
Well, that's kind of mean.

00:05:28.367 --> 00:05:32.534
[ Laughter ]
But it's funny.

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:34.567
-And you've done stand-up.

00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.934
You've done tours I know
with Tom Davis.

00:05:36.934 --> 00:05:38.767
Is this your first
solo comedy --

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:40.834
-It's my first time
doing it solo.

00:05:40.834 --> 00:05:43.300
I started -- I love stand-up.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:45.467
You know.
You're a great stand-up.

00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:47.567
-I love stand-up.
-And how much fun it is.

00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:51.167
So I worked up a -- I have a --
I'm great.

00:05:51.167 --> 00:05:52.834
It's really hilarious.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:52.834 --> 00:05:57.567
I did 15 cities in the fall,
and I was booked for 16 more.

00:05:57.567 --> 00:05:59.934
I'm going to Portland, Maine,
this Sunday.

00:05:59.934 --> 00:06:01.100
-It's a beautiful place.

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:03.300
And also, are you enjoying
the podcast?

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.934
-I love doing the podcast.

00:06:04.934 --> 00:06:08.200
The podcast is really
public policy,

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:10.200
politics but also comedy.

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:16.767
So, we've had guests like
Steven Schmidt, Anthony Fauci,

00:06:16.767 --> 00:06:20.133
Sarah Silverman, Conan O'Brien.

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:22.467
Michelle Obama.
-Wow.

00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:25.000
-Well, not Michelle Obama
but guests like her.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.667
[ Laughter ]

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:29.801
-Can you say that in your promo?

00:06:29.801 --> 00:06:32.467
Guests like Michelle --
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:32.467 --> 00:06:33.534
-Yeah, of course.

00:06:33.534 --> 00:06:35.234
-Just like.
-It's very good.

00:06:35.234 --> 00:06:38.567
It's informative.
But I got to say, it's funny.

00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:40.367
You're so funny.
-Thank you.

00:06:40.367 --> 00:06:41.834
-You can't not be funny.

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:43.868
I'm so happy that you're here
on the show,

00:06:43.868 --> 00:06:45.501
and good luck with
the rest of the tour.

00:06:45.501 --> 00:06:47.334
-Thanks for having me.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:47.334 --> 00:06:48.667
Thank you, everybody.

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:50.334
-That's Al Franken, everybody.

00:06:50.334 --> 00:06:51.667
Go see Al on tour.

00:06:51.667 --> 00:06:54.634
Go to alfranken.com for tickets.

